WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.390
The DARPA Urban
Challenge promises

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.940
to be a stage for innovation
in the areas of robotics,

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.870
perception, and planning.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.070
MIT has assembled a team of
top researchers in these fields

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.530
and is determined to
win the Grand Challenge.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.020
With that in mind, MIT
has chosen the Land Rover

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.590
LR3 because of its unique on-
and off-road capabilities.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.200
The vehicle will rely upon a
layered sensor architecture

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.300
that will incorporate
a coupled GPS/INS

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.490
system, a series
of redundant LiDAR

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.040
scanners for short- to
medium-range sensing,

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.530
and several telephoto and
wide-field cameras that

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.910
will be used for object
detection in the near and far

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:33.920
field.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.080
While capable
hardware is necessary,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.910
the unique nature of
the upcoming challenge

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.760
places the greatest
responsibility on software,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.190
and winning will require a
revolution in the approach

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.640
to the problems, ranging
from perception to planning.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.230
Whether you are in
Baghdad or Boston,

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.230
uncertainty is implicit in
all aspects of urban driving.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.810
The driver must constantly be
aware of a pedestrian darting

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.390
out onto the street or the
car ahead suddenly braking,

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.220
as well as replan
routes on the fly

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.650
to deal with unexpected
road construction.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.200
Uncertainty will similarly
extend to all aspects

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.709
of the DARPA Grand Challenge.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:07.070
MIT will take advantage
of its unique research

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.680
and statistical state
estimation and mapping,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.740
planning under uncertainty,
and statistical perception

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.110
in developing a software
architecture that addresses

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.960
uncertainty at all levels
of the perception, planning,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.520
and control tasks.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.830
At the high level,
our architecture

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.160
will include a mission planner
that manages the global plan,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.920
a situational planner that
is designed to rapidly handle

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.870
the uncertainty of
the environment,

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:31.580
and a perceptual
state estimator that

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.580
extracts salient
information from the scene.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.710
A map fragment database
maintains a history

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.850
of the relevant scene
structure and allows

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.850
the technical planner to
be more aggressive when

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.410
revisiting areas.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.750
This simulation shows a
motion planning technique

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.820
applied to a sample RNDF.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.740
Under nominal
conditions, the planner

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.570
chooses the shortest
path to the goal,

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.290
but is able to replant on the
fly when an obstruction arises.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.870
In the multi-vehicle
case, our algorithms

00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:00.050
predict the motion of other
agents and replan short horizon

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.230
trajectories in order to
avoid conflicting paths.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.570
The path planning relies
on a condensed description

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.940
of the environment
provided by the sensors.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.009
Similarly, our current
vision research

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.650
is capable of real-time,
3D tracking of features

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.740
and shapes, which then provides
rich and salient information

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.030
about the environment.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.460
With video, we identify
long-range movement

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.140
with particles
that capture motion

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.230
that is spatially dense
and temporally long-range.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.790
The obstacles can
then be tracked

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.530
using robust and
optimized vision

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.780
methods that take into account
the structure of the scene.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.840
In this mockup, the
system separates the road

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.570
shown in blue from the obstacles
shown in red and extracts

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.420
in the far field a sign which,
in turn, impacts the driving

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:39.350
behavior.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.690
Additionally, the mapping
component of this system

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.150
will draw upon our current
research in localization

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.160
and mapping, which
has been demonstrated

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.640
in large, complex environments.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.490
MIT, in conjunction
with Olin College,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.490
currently has the skills
in hardware development

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.290
and construction
necessary to implement

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.820
these unique algorithms.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.880
Work by team members previously
at the iRobot corporation

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.540
demonstrates the ability to
deploy autonomous vehicles

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.980
that operate in rugged,
outdoor terrain.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.300
Common both to on-road
and urban driving,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.120
puddles are one
challenging feature,

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.210
as they are not easily detected
by standard laser range-finder

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.380
technology.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.990
Driving in an urban environment
will require highly precise

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.940
navigation at the local scale.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.820
Using the NavCom system, we can
accurately control the vehicle

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.430
based upon an IMU and by
directly incorporating

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.810
occasional GPS data, can
achieve centimeter precision.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.908
Here, the system uses
the vehicle's own motion

00:03:27.908 --> 00:03:30.200
to recover three-dimensional
information from the plane

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.420
or laser range data
of the vehicle.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.760
Some vehicle poses can cause
a ground plane to appear

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.247
as an obstacle to the system.

00:03:36.247 --> 00:03:38.330
Here, we show that the
vehicle slows down in order

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.040
to ensure that the
perceived obstacle

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.082
during the downward pitch
is, in fact, the ground

00:03:42.082 --> 00:03:44.387
plane before continuing
at high speed.

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:46.220
Part of the DARPA
Challenge requires ability

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.800
to park between stationary,
unmodeled vehicles.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.440
Here, we see a vehicle
demonstrating a similar ability

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.140
by autonomously pulling into
an orchard lane using a fusion

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.380
GPS/LiDAR wheel
odometer InVision.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.810
GPS is used to identify
the approximate location

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.540
of the lane opening, after
which local sensors take over

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.840
for navigation.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.080
Another critical capability
required for the DARPA

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.700
Challenge is
sensing and tracking

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.350
of other moving vehicles.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.720
Here, we see the ATV
tracking the moving vehicles

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.420
ahead of it using only a
single laser range-finder

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.890
and a color camera.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.380
MIT has a long-standing
history of building autonomous

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.720
platforms for a wide
range of environments

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.480
that have successfully helped
to pave the way for the maturing

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.063
field of robotics.

00:04:25.063 --> 00:04:26.480
We are looking
forward to applying

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.510
our technical experience to
the 2006 DARPA Urban Challenge.

